@meta
id: 28-15
title: Proving it is your config
chapter: 28
type: lesson
skills: vimrc-debug
par: 100
minutes: 7
vscode: "Extensions: Disable All and reload", the same technique with a button
open: .vimrc

@brief
Something is inserting tab characters into your TypeScript, and you have
had three settings in your config for about a week. Before you change
anything, four commands answer the questions in order.

`vim --clean` starts vim with no config at all. If the problem is gone,
it is yours. `vim -u NONE` goes further — no config, no plugins, no
filetype detection — which is the baseline the manual means when it says
"try it with -u NONE". That is question one: mine or vim's?

`:scriptnames` lists every file vim sourced, in order. Question two:
what is even loaded? It is where you discover that the config you have
been editing is not the one being read.

`:verbose set expandtab?` prints the value *and* the file and line that
last set it. Question three: who did this? That one command replaces most
config debugging. It works for mappings too: `:verbose nmap <leader>w`.

And when none of those pin it down, you bisect. Comment out half the
config, source it, and see which half the problem lives in. Halving
beats reading, every time.

Do the bisect step here. The three lines under `Experiments` are the
newest thing in the file and the only part you cannot account for. Leave
a note saying why, then comment all three out:

    " --- Experiments ---
    " bisecting ORD-902: everything below is off until the tabs stop
    " set noexpandtab
    " set foldmethod=indent
    " set conceallevel=2

@keys
| `vim --clean` | start with no config |
| `vim -u NONE` | no config, no plugins, no filetype detection |
| `:scriptnames` | every file vim has sourced, in order |
| `:verbose set expandtab?` | the value, and who set it last |
| `:18,20s/^/" /` | comment out a range of lines |

@hints
Write the note first. `O` from a line that is not yet a comment gives
you a clean line to type on.
--
`set noexpandtab` is on line 17 before you add anything. `17G` gets you
there without counting.
--
After the note, the three settings have moved down to lines 18, 19 and
20. `:18,20s/^/" /` puts a quote and a space at the start of each.

@solution
    17G O " bisecting ORD-902: everything below is off until the tabs stop <Esc>
    :18,20s/^/" /<Enter> :wq<Enter>

`s/^/" /` over a range is the whole bisecting technique. A visual block
and `I"` does the same job; so does `:18,20 normal I" `. Use whichever
you reach for fastest, because you will do it often and always in a bad
mood.

Then source or restart, and you have halved the problem in one step.
Three lines is a small enough half to finish by eye — `noexpandtab` is
the one, because it is global and it is not a TypeScript setting at all.
`:verbose set expandtab?` would have said so directly: *Last set from
~/.vimrc line 17*.

The bit worth internalising is the order. Reproduce it with `--clean`,
find out what is loaded with `:scriptnames`, ask who set it with
`:verbose`, and only then start commenting things out. People do it in
the opposite order and spend an evening on it.

One more habit: when a line has served its purpose, delete it rather
than leaving it commented out forever. A config full of commented-out
settings is a config you have stopped trusting, and every one of them is
a question you will re-ask in six months.

@start .vimrc
" ~/.vimrc - one line at a time, when something annoys you.

set nocompatible
syntax on
filetype plugin indent on

" --- Indentation ---
set autoindent
set expandtab
set shiftwidth=2

" --- Editing ---
set hidden
set backspace=indent,eol,start

" --- Experiments ---
set noexpandtab
set foldmethod=indent
set conceallevel=2

@expected .vimrc
" ~/.vimrc - one line at a time, when something annoys you.

set nocompatible
syntax on
filetype plugin indent on

" --- Indentation ---
set autoindent
set expandtab
set shiftwidth=2

" --- Editing ---
set hidden
set backspace=indent,eol,start

" --- Experiments ---
" bisecting ORD-902: everything below is off until the tabs stop
" set noexpandtab
" set foldmethod=indent
" set conceallevel=2
